<speak>
<break time="1500ms"/>
<mark name="b1_promise"/>
Sixteen hours of engineering work, finished in twelve minutes. Here's how.
<break time="1500ms"/>
<mark name="b2_ticket"/>
An engineer defines the outcome in Jira — the user story, acceptance criteria. One label hands it to OutcomeOps.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b3_webhook"/>
Jira fires an automation rule. An SSM document invokes the generation Lambda — entirely inside the customer's AWS account.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b4_rag"/>
The Lambda queries the team's knowledge base. Vector search plus Cohere rerank picks the top ADRs for context.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b5_plan"/>
OutcomeOps writes a plan — eight atomic steps, each bound to specific files and ADRs. The plan itself is committed to the branch.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b6a_loop"/>
The plan feeds itself through SQS. One step, one Lambda. Generate. Write. Enqueue the next.
<break time="4s"/>
<mark name="b6b_norunner"/>
No orchestrator. No conductor. Just a queue, a worker, and a plan.
<break time="3s"/>
<mark name="b6c_files"/>
Eight steps later: models, handler, infrastructure, tests — each written to the team's standards.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b7_pr"/>
Eight files. Three hundred forty-two lines. One pull request, signed by the bot.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b8_analyzer"/>
A second RAG pass. The analyzer re-queries the same ADRs and validates the generated code. Six checks pass, before a human looks at it.
<break time="1s"/>
<mark name="b9_close"/>
Sixteen hours of engineering, done in twelve minutes — for a dollar seventeen. Context engineered.
<break time="2s"/>
</speak>
